---
import HomeIcon from '@/components/HomeIcon.astro';
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About — Estelle Sweeney" description="Estelle Sweeney is a New York-based photographer, artist, and art director working across runway, backstage, editorial, and fine art.">
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-black text-cream">

    <nav class="flex items-center justify-between px-8 py-6 border-b border-white/10">
      <HomeIcon />
      <a href="mailto:estellescreative@gmail.com" class="text-xs tracking-[0.15em] uppercase font-display hover:opacity-50 transition-opacity">Contact</a>
    </nav>

    <main class="px-8 py-14 max-w-3xl mx-auto">

      {/* Header */}
      <h1 class="font-display text-sm tracking-[0.3em] uppercase pb-8 border-b border-white/10 mb-12">About</h1>

      {/* Ethos */}
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-8 mb-20 pb-16 border-b border-white/10" style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/90">
          I am interested in people.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          Photography has never been about documenting appearances for me. It is a way of searching for the invisible. I am drawn to the stories people carry beneath the surface, the emotions that live in a glance, a gesture, the weight of tired hands, or the quiet moment when someone forgets they are being watched. Those are the moments when the mask falls away and something more honest begins to emerge. The camera is my medium, just like paint on a paintbrush. The only difference is I paint with light.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          Fashion became a natural place for this search because it gathers people who are deeply devoted to making. Designers, models, musicians, artisans, stylists, writers, photographers, and filmmakers all come together to create something that exists for only a brief moment. I am fascinated by that shared energy. The friendships, the trust, the obsession, and the willingness to pour everything into something fleeting. What interests me is not perfection but presence.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          The photographs I return to are rarely the obvious ones. They happen between performances. A model lost in thought before stepping onto the runway. Hands unconsciously adjusting a garment. Feet carrying the excitement of a fantastic day. A distant stare that belongs to an entirely different story. These moments reveal something deeper than performance. They remind us that every person contains a world far larger than what can be seen.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          I work across photography, film, sculpture, graphic design, installation, and jewelry because each medium uncovers a different way of understanding an idea. I am less interested in staying within disciplines — I want them to be allowed to overlap. The most meaningful work often exists where boundaries dissolve, where fashion meets fine art, documentary meets fiction, and traditional craft meets new technology.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          Travel has become one of my greatest teachers. Every city carries its own mythology, every landscape its own rhythm, and every community its own way of seeing the world. I collect these encounters through photographs, conversations, research, found objects, and memory. Together they become an evolving archive that continues to shape the way I see people and places.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          I am drawn to transformation, to imperfect textures, grain, movement, darkness, tenderness, contradiction, and the beauty of what is overlooked. I believe the most powerful images leave room for mystery. They invite us to slow down, look again, and discover something of ourselves within them.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/80">
          At its core, my practice is about revealing the worlds that already exist around us. The communities we build, the rituals we inherit, the histories we carry, and the unseen connections that shape our lives. Every project is an attempt to make those invisible worlds visible, and to remind us that every person is living a story far deeper than the one we first imagine.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Practice areas */}
      <div class="mb-16 border-t border-white/10 pt-10">
        <h2 class="text-sm tracking-[0.25em] uppercase font-display text-white/40 mb-8">Practice</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-white/10" style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
          <li class="py-4 flex items-baseline justify-between">
            <span class="text-base">Runway &amp; Backstage</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-white/40 uppercase tracking-wider">NYFW · PFW</span>
          </li>
          <li class="py-4 flex items-baseline justify-between">
            <span class="text-base">Editorial &amp; Campaign</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-white/40 uppercase tracking-wider">Analog + Digital</span>
          </li>
          <li class="py-4 flex items-baseline justify-between">
            <span class="text-base">Fine Art &amp; Installation</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-white/40 uppercase tracking-wider">Mixed Media</span>
          </li>
          <li class="py-4 flex items-baseline justify-between">
            <span class="text-base">Graphic Design</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-white/40 uppercase tracking-wider">Art Direction</span>
          </li>
          <li class="py-4 flex items-baseline justify-between">
            <span class="text-base">Pame Studio</span>
            <span class="text-sm text-white/40 uppercase tracking-wider">Jewelry</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      {/* Approach */}
      <div class="mb-16 border-t border-white/10 pt-10">
        <h2 class="text-sm tracking-[0.25em] uppercase font-display text-white/40 mb-8">Approach</h2>
        <p class="text-base leading-loose text-white/75" style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
          Shot on Portra 400, Portra 800, and Ilford — with a Canon R6 alongside for situations that won't wait.
          The work is cinematic and archival by instinct: raw access, imperfect moments, images that look like they've always existed.
          Fashion and fine art don't live in separate rooms here. They're the same practice.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Contact CTA */}
